1
00:00:07,790 --> 00:00:12,509
I've been very lucky to be able to study storytelling from many different perspectives.

2
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:17,870
While I lived in Los Angeles in America, while I was there, I ran a production company called

3
00:00:17,870 --> 00:00:21,769
Luminary Productions. And now we've got a branch of it here in Australia as well.

4
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:25,690
And because of this, I got to learn storytelling from the perspective of

5
00:00:25,690 --> 00:00:30,449
videographers and directors. I got to learn how they storytell visually.

6
00:00:30,910 --> 00:00:34,329
Now, more than this, I also learned about storytelling in the world of magic,

7
00:00:34,329 --> 00:00:38,789
how storytelling can enhance magic and bring the magic to life.

8
00:00:39,329 --> 00:00:42,509
And then I finally learned storytelling as a keynote speaker,

9
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:47,369
as someone who storytells on stage with thousands of people in the audience often.

10
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:51,810
So I've been able to look at storytelling from many different perspectives.

11
00:00:52,829 --> 00:00:57,250
And what I've been able to do is I've been able to learn all the different types of storytelling

12
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:02,429
formulas. And after learning them all, you've got seven-step formulas, 10-step formulas,

13
00:01:02,670 --> 00:01:04,310
20-plus-step formulas.

14
00:01:04,329 --> 00:01:09,269
Especially when you're scripting for a movie. And I realized that the more complex a formula,

15
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:15,010
the less likely you are to actually follow it, and the less likely you are to even use it.

16
00:01:15,390 --> 00:01:20,489
So to me, I wanted to find the most distilled version of a storytelling formula, and I've

17
00:01:20,489 --> 00:01:25,129
discovered it. So the storytelling formula I'm going to share with you, there are only three

18
00:01:25,129 --> 00:01:32,250
steps. But please don't confuse simplicity for stupidity. Simplicity, again, is distilled power.

19
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:34,030
It's distilled wisdom.

20
00:01:34,329 --> 00:01:39,769
Now, the first step when it comes to great storytelling, step one is called the incident.

21
00:01:40,430 --> 00:01:44,209
The incident. Now, the incident is made up of many ingredients.

22
00:01:44,450 --> 00:01:45,450
And here are the ingredients.

23
00:01:45,950 --> 00:01:50,549
It's made up of the who, the where, the what and the when.

24
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:54,870
These are the basic ingredients you must have when you storytell.

25
00:01:55,489 --> 00:01:58,450
Now, a lot of storytellers have the who, what, where and when.

26
00:01:58,590 --> 00:02:01,730
But they don't have the next two ingredients when they're setting up the incident.

27
00:02:01,969 --> 00:02:03,950
And when you don't have the next two ingredients.

28
00:02:04,329 --> 00:02:09,590
you're going to just be an okay storyteller. Whereas if you want to be a great storyteller,

29
00:02:09,669 --> 00:02:15,669
you've got to have the next two ingredients. Now, the next one is you want to include VACS,

30
00:02:15,770 --> 00:02:23,289
V-A-K-S. And when you include VACS, this is when you add color to the story. And what do I mean by

31
00:02:23,289 --> 00:02:32,349
VACS? The V in VACS stands for visual, the A stands for auditory, the K stands for kinesthetic,

32
00:02:32,349 --> 00:02:39,009
and the S stands for smell. This is how you add color to the story and start to bring the story

33
00:02:39,009 --> 00:02:44,610
to life. Again, when you think about the story I told of how I met my wife, with that particular

34
00:02:44,610 --> 00:02:50,050
story, I told you the visuals. I described to you it was a two-level club. You know, there's the

35
00:02:50,050 --> 00:02:54,889
bottom club, the bottom level for, you know, the normal folk, and then the upper one for the high

36
00:02:54,889 --> 00:03:00,409
roller folk. And then after that, I also told you about a bit of the auditory, loud music, kinesthetic,

37
00:03:00,469 --> 00:03:02,330
how I felt. Oh, I was feeling nervous.

38
00:03:02,349 --> 00:03:06,830
Oh my goodness, when she saw me, I lost all my confidence. And then I even quickly touched on

39
00:03:06,830 --> 00:03:11,629
smell when we were standing next to the toilets and it smelled a bit like pee. Crazy things that

40
00:03:11,629 --> 00:03:17,449
I like to point out, but it actually smelled pretty bad. And that's me using VACS just to

41
00:03:17,449 --> 00:03:22,030
quickly add a little bit of color to the story. And the final ingredient that we must add if we

42
00:03:22,030 --> 00:03:30,770
want to become great storytellers is what is known as bringing the characters to life. You want to

43
00:03:30,770 --> 00:03:32,330
bring the characters to life.

44
00:03:32,349 --> 00:03:38,550
Now, the way you can do this is you can use two methods to do this. You can bring them to life with your body

45
00:03:38,550 --> 00:03:44,689
language, and then you can also bring the characters to life with dialogue. Body language and dialogue.

46
00:03:45,490 --> 00:03:50,789
If you recall the story of How I Met My Wife Again, I was bringing the characters to life. When I played

47
00:03:50,789 --> 00:03:57,189
myself and my wife, I played myself, you know, walking all kind of, you know, all confident. And then when

48
00:03:57,189 --> 00:04:01,610
she looked at me, I kind of lost all my confidence. I showed you that with my body language.

49
00:04:01,610 --> 00:04:06,770
And then I played myself in one spot. I said, you know, would you be impressed if I transform the King of Hearts

50
00:04:06,770 --> 00:04:13,270
into the Queen of Hearts? And then I played my wife. So I switched over and played my wife's character, and I

51
00:04:13,270 --> 00:04:19,509
brought her to life with my body language. Now, more than this, I brought in dialogue. I was able to bring myself

52
00:04:19,509 --> 00:04:25,870
to life by doing that cheesy voice there. Would you be impressed? I brought myself to life with dialogue in my

53
00:04:25,870 --> 00:04:31,430
voice. And then I did my wife's voice as well. I did the whole higher pitch voice. I went there. I

54
00:04:31,610 --> 00:04:37,050
said, would you be impressed if I transformed yourself into a real man? As opposed to, imagine I told the story and I said,

55
00:04:37,069 --> 00:04:42,670
I played myself and I didn't do the nervous voice and I just said, so I said to her, would you be impressed if I

56
00:04:42,670 --> 00:04:47,389
transform a King of Hearts into the Queen of Hearts? You know, and then she looked at me and she said, I'd be more

57
00:04:47,389 --> 00:04:53,829
impressed if you transformed yourself into a real man. Like again, it just loses its power. And when you bring the

58
00:04:53,829 --> 00:05:01,470
characters to life with body language and dialogue, what happens is you go from reporting a story to reliving

59
00:05:01,610 --> 00:05:08,610
a story. Most people, when they storytell, they just report stories. And the difference between reporting and

60
00:05:08,610 --> 00:05:17,870
reliving is you bringing the characters to life. That's how you relive a story. Otherwise, if I was reporting the

61
00:05:17,870 --> 00:05:24,490
story, all I'd say is, you know, I met my wife 10 years ago. We met at a bar called Distill. I showed her a quick magic

62
00:05:24,490 --> 00:05:29,470
trick. She didn't like it. So to me, I just tried to come back again to see if I could court her and see her again. And I

63
00:05:29,470 --> 00:05:31,430
did. And the final time I saw her,

64
00:05:31,610 --> 00:05:37,470
instead of showing her a magic trick, we went out for a coffee and we got married. You see, I'm still using my instrument

65
00:05:37,470 --> 00:05:44,050
well, but I'm not following the ingredients. I just had the who, what, where and when, didn't have any vacs, and I didn't

66
00:05:44,050 --> 00:05:50,629
bring the characters to life. I hope you can see the difference now and a clear difference between how most people just

67
00:05:50,629 --> 00:05:58,230
report stories. If you want to become an exceptional storyteller, you want to relive them. Now, there are two problems

68
00:05:58,230 --> 00:06:00,870
when it comes to storytelling. Two problems.

69
00:06:02,689 --> 00:06:12,129
Problems. Now, problem number one is that you take too long to set up the incident. You take way too long. That's what I used to do.

70
00:06:12,209 --> 00:06:19,910
This was something that I was guilty of. When I first met my wife and we started going out, we started dating more. I used to

71
00:06:19,910 --> 00:06:27,310
storytelling a way that causes her to always go, Oh, my goodness, what is the point of this story? But she'd never say that, of

72
00:06:27,310 --> 00:06:30,569
course, because when we were dating, we're still trying to, you know, court each other.

73
00:06:31,769 --> 00:06:39,189
But now that we're married, it drives her insane. And often when I storytell before I learned this formula well, she'd often go, Oh, can you

74
00:06:39,189 --> 00:06:47,110
just, you know, can you get to the point? And that's the problem is that when we take too long to set up the incident, people lose

75
00:06:47,110 --> 00:06:54,029
interest. And I used to get caught up in this. I used to take too much time describing exactly where I was describing the room. And oh, by

76
00:06:54,029 --> 00:06:59,129
the way, that room had a really cool painting that had a playing card in it. Isn't it so weird it had a playing card in it? Why would it

77
00:06:59,129 --> 00:07:00,050
have a playing card in it?

78
00:07:00,149 --> 00:07:01,589
I wonder if it's a magic related thing.

79
00:07:01,610 --> 00:07:08,910
Yeah, that's the problem with painting. And then I just take too long to get to the point of the story. So the first problem is that it takes too long. It takes too long.

80
00:07:09,110 --> 00:07:19,069
Now, the second problem is that it's too short. And this is how I know my wife and I are perfect for each other, is that I take too long to tell a story.

81
00:07:19,629 --> 00:07:26,930
She doesn't give me enough of the ingredients. She'll just tell me a story. And then she'll just go, and that's why we're never going to that grocery store again.

82
00:07:27,709 --> 00:07:31,189
And I kind of go, I you know, I was listening.

83
00:07:31,610 --> 00:07:35,009
Because you always tell me that I'm not listening, but I don't even know why we're not going to that grocery store,

84
00:07:35,050 --> 00:07:36,230
because that's my favorite grocery store.

85
00:07:36,829 --> 00:07:39,829
And then she doesn't give me enough information. She doesn't give me enough context.

86
00:07:40,470 --> 00:07:43,889
So you've got to make sure that it's not too long and it's not too short.

87
00:07:44,129 --> 00:07:48,709
You want to make sure you've got enough of the ingredients so that you can bring the story to life,

88
00:07:48,769 --> 00:07:52,269
but not too much so that you end up suffocating the story.

89
00:07:52,589 --> 00:07:54,529
So not too much and not too little.

90
00:07:54,829 --> 00:08:00,829
And the ideal time that I've discovered as a keynote speaker and as someone who uses stories often at dinner

91
00:08:00,829 --> 00:08:05,550
and gatherings is that the optimal time for a story is about two to three minutes.

92
00:08:05,829 --> 00:08:08,009
This is the optimal time for a beginner.

93
00:08:08,509 --> 00:08:13,050
As you're just starting out your journey as a storyteller, around two to three minutes,

94
00:08:13,129 --> 00:08:16,350
setting up the incident, that's acceptable. That's okay.

95
00:08:17,089 --> 00:08:21,649
Now, if you want to see some expert storytellers, just look up some stand-up comedians.

96
00:08:21,769 --> 00:08:25,829
And when you look up these stand-up comedians, you'll notice that they can tell a story.

97
00:08:26,009 --> 00:08:30,569
They can set up the incident, bring the characters to life, add the vax, who, what, where and when,

98
00:08:30,829 --> 00:08:33,690
they can do this often in 30-second bits.

99
00:08:33,950 --> 00:08:40,409
And it takes them a year, months to distill their storytelling down to 30 seconds.

100
00:08:40,809 --> 00:08:43,629
So don't hold yourself to the same level of expectation.

101
00:08:44,070 --> 00:08:51,549
We can move towards shortening the story, getting it to maybe two minutes, closer to two minutes, maybe a minute.

102
00:08:51,929 --> 00:08:56,610
And understand that if you ever want to get it down to 30 seconds, like stand-up comedians,

103
00:08:56,750 --> 00:09:00,409
that's going to take a lot of polishing and a lot of practice.

104
00:09:01,350 --> 00:09:03,669
That's the first step for the incident.

105
00:09:03,929 --> 00:09:10,330
Now, the second step is, I've already kind of mentioned it, but the second step in storytelling is the point.

106
00:09:11,169 --> 00:09:14,009
What's the point of the story? What is the point?

107
00:09:14,409 --> 00:09:20,750
I mean, when you're storytelling, if you don't have a point, this is one of the things that annoys people the most,

108
00:09:20,909 --> 00:09:23,269
especially when you're using it in a professional context.

109
00:09:23,669 --> 00:09:27,870
Sometimes you might be storytelling to a friend and the whole purpose of it is just because it was entertaining.

110
00:09:28,230 --> 00:09:28,870
That's fine.

111
00:09:29,149 --> 00:09:30,809
But if you're using storytelling...

112
00:09:30,830 --> 00:09:34,809
If you're using storytelling in a professional context and you don't have a point, it drives people crazy.

113
00:09:34,909 --> 00:09:38,309
And that's when people think and have the perception that storytelling is unprofessional.

114
00:09:38,710 --> 00:09:46,289
It's not because storytelling is unprofessional, it's because the storytelling that they've experienced so far in a professional setting wasn't very good.

115
00:09:46,730 --> 00:09:50,110
And you've got to make sure a part of that is that you've got a point to your story.

116
00:09:50,330 --> 00:09:54,149
And this is how you can let people know that the point is about to arrive.

117
00:09:54,509 --> 00:09:58,509
You use this sentence to let people know that this is the point of the story.

118
00:09:58,669 --> 00:09:59,649
This is what you say.

119
00:09:59,750 --> 00:10:00,730
You say the following.

120
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:06,519
The reason I'm telling you this is because...

121
00:10:07,139 --> 00:10:12,639
This line is psychologically going to get people to pay more attention the moment you say it.

122
00:10:12,720 --> 00:10:15,779
This is a way to pique their interest and pique their attention.

123
00:10:16,080 --> 00:10:20,000
And the reason this piques their interest is because when you say the word,

124
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:26,720
the reason I'm telling you this is because it lets them know you're about to turn information into insight.

125
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:29,779
You're about to turn information into insight.

126
00:10:30,059 --> 00:10:31,779
And this is how people know to...

127
00:10:31,779 --> 00:10:36,399
To increase their levels of attention because most people want to know why you're telling them this story.

128
00:10:36,740 --> 00:10:38,379
They're thinking that in the back of their head.

129
00:10:38,679 --> 00:10:39,379
They're thinking...

130
00:10:39,919 --> 00:10:41,580
The moment you start storytelling, they're thinking,

131
00:10:41,679 --> 00:10:43,179
Why are you telling me this story?

132
00:10:43,539 --> 00:10:46,740
Now, if you take too long, they start to get annoyed and go,

133
00:10:46,820 --> 00:10:48,539
I don't understand why this person is telling me this story.

134
00:10:48,940 --> 00:10:51,360
But if you take about two to three minutes to tell the story,

135
00:10:51,580 --> 00:10:53,840
it makes them very engaged because they're thinking,

136
00:10:53,899 --> 00:10:55,860
I wonder why this person is telling me the story.

137
00:10:56,279 --> 00:10:58,799
And this, at this level of interest, it's still positive.

138
00:10:59,120 --> 00:11:01,399
Whereas if you take too long, it turns into negative interest.

139
00:11:01,399 --> 00:11:02,799
But in the beginning, it's kind of like,

140
00:11:02,840 --> 00:11:03,960
Oh, I wonder why they're telling me.

141
00:11:04,019 --> 00:11:05,320
And then when you say the line,

142
00:11:05,399 --> 00:11:07,919
The reason I'm telling you the story is because...

143
00:11:07,919 --> 00:11:08,179
They're like,

144
00:11:08,220 --> 00:11:09,440
Oh, here we go.

145
00:11:09,759 --> 00:11:11,440
I'm about to get context as to why.

146
00:11:12,019 --> 00:11:14,720
And then that's where you have them hooked.

147
00:11:15,259 --> 00:11:19,720
Another quick reason why this is so powerful is simply because you've got the word you in there.

148
00:11:19,899 --> 00:11:24,659
And, you know, we've all heard about people saying when you're standing there and talking to an audience,

149
00:11:24,899 --> 00:11:25,759
they're just thinking,

150
00:11:25,879 --> 00:11:26,919
What's in it for me?

151
00:11:27,179 --> 00:11:28,039
What's in it for me?

152
00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:29,100
What's in it for me?

153
00:11:29,340 --> 00:11:30,019
And it's true.

154
00:11:30,100 --> 00:11:31,379
That's what they're most likely thinking.

155
00:11:31,399 --> 00:11:33,460
So by you using the word you,

156
00:11:33,620 --> 00:11:37,559
the reason I'm telling you this is because it just makes them engage more.

157
00:11:37,960 --> 00:11:41,379
These are the first two steps when it comes to great storytelling.

158
00:11:41,879 --> 00:11:43,340
Now, before you go any further,

159
00:11:43,419 --> 00:11:45,139
before we go to the final step of storytelling,

160
00:11:45,419 --> 00:11:47,799
I want you to find a story in your life.

161
00:11:47,899 --> 00:11:49,120
Any story will do for now.

162
00:11:49,259 --> 00:11:52,659
And then I want you to take that story and plug it into this formula.

163
00:11:52,940 --> 00:11:56,240
And remember, when you're setting up the incident and you're taking the story

164
00:11:56,240 --> 00:11:58,320
and you're making sure you've got all the necessary ingredients,

165
00:11:58,559 --> 00:12:01,379
really push yourself with bringing the story.

166
00:12:01,399 --> 00:12:02,240
Bring the characters to life.

167
00:12:02,360 --> 00:12:04,519
Make sure you include some dialogue.

168
00:12:04,740 --> 00:12:06,980
Find some opportunities to include some dialogue.

169
00:12:07,419 --> 00:12:09,379
And make sure you bring them to life.

170
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:12,600
This is the opportunity for you to break the mold

171
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:14,639
and not play within your comfort zone,

172
00:12:14,779 --> 00:12:16,899
but push yourself outside of your comfort zone

173
00:12:16,899 --> 00:12:19,360
if you normally don't bring the characters to life.

174
00:12:19,559 --> 00:12:21,259
Make sure you've got a bit of ax there too.

175
00:12:21,740 --> 00:12:23,500
And here is where you double check.

176
00:12:23,679 --> 00:12:26,899
Are you taking longer than two to three minutes to set up the incident?

177
00:12:27,440 --> 00:12:29,860
And the final one that you learned was about the point.

178
00:12:31,399 --> 00:12:33,039
When you start with your story, you use the sentence,

179
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:35,139
the reason I'm telling you this is because.

180
00:12:35,919 --> 00:12:39,279
When people don't do this, they tend to waffle on with their stories.

181
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:43,120
They tend to, oh yeah, by the way, let me tell you about who else was there.

182
00:12:43,179 --> 00:12:46,399
I was with my good friend Lenny and then, oh, let me tell you about Lenny.

183
00:12:46,740 --> 00:12:51,399
What happens is this is when you see people go off on tangents and start to ramble.

184
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:52,940
Use this sentence.

185
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:57,139
It helps you take off well with the plane and then land the plane beautifully,

186
00:12:57,539 --> 00:12:58,659
if you would, as a metaphor.

187
00:12:58,980 --> 00:13:01,340
It just helps you tie a nice bow on the story

188
00:13:01,399 --> 00:13:05,200
by using the sentence, the reason I'm telling you this is because.

189
00:13:06,100 --> 00:13:09,700
Spend a bit of time taking the story and plugging it into this formula.

190
00:13:10,159 --> 00:13:11,940
Don't go ahead until you've done this.

191
00:13:12,580 --> 00:13:13,700
See you in the next video.

